WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
Most people think they're using AI well

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:07.480
when they get a decent answer from chat

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.400
GPT. That was enough 6 months ago. It's

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:12.160
not enough anymore. The next shift is AI

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.400
agents, and [music] the gap between

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.640
people who understand them and people

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.640
who don't, it's about to get very

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.520
expensive. I've spent years in the

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:23.600
boardrooms of billion-dollar companies,

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:26.120
and the good news here is that agents

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.280
are much simpler than most people think.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:31.040
So, in this video, I'll show you exactly

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:33.520
how they work, when to use them, and how

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:37.000
to start before everyone else catches

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:39.360
up. An AI prompt and an AI agent are

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.920
completely different, but most of us are

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.120
still stuck with old habits. Let me give

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.360
you the simplest way to think about this

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:48.200
before we go any further. This is our

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:51.920
first framework, right off the bat. I

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:54.560
call it ARR. If a task is autonomous,

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.280
recurring, and reviewable, it's a strong

00:00:54.560 --> 00:01:00.520
candidate for an agent. If it needs live

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:03.160
judgment, or it only happens once, or

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:05.880
can't be reviewed clearly, then use a

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:08.520
prompt. That one distinction alone will

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:11.320
put you miles ahead of most people using

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:14.320
AI today. The internet gave us search,

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:17.040
so we started Googling. AI gave us LLMs,

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:20.480
or large language models, but most

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:23.440
people today still think of AI as a

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:25.280
glorified search. Now, agents are here,

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:28.280
and we're still making the same mistake

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:31.800
again. We think of agents as just more

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:34.760
capable chatbots. They're not. A chatbot

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:37.600
waits for your next prompt. An agent

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:39.840
figures out its next move. Prompting is

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.040
like sitting next to a student driver.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.440
You still have to guide them, correct

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:46.560
them, and stay very alert. An agent, on

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:49.280
the other hand, is a hired driver. You

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.400
set the destination, hand over the keys,

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.400
and just sit in the backseat. It handles

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:55.720
the route, the traffic, and all the

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:58.440
step-by-step decisions. That's the

00:01:55.720 --> 00:02:01.040
mental shift we have to make. So, here's

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:02.600
a prompt. Write me a LinkedIn post and

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.800
here's an agent. Watch my [music]

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:07.920
industry every Monday, find the three

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:10.200
most relevant stories, study my previous

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.880
post, draft a new post based on those

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.480
stories in my voice, revise against my

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:18.400
style, and schedule it for Tuesday

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:20.880
morning. That's the power of AI agents.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:23.000
And to wield that power, you need to

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.160
know what's actually running under the

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.280
hood. Everyone's talking about AI

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:29.000
agents. Almost nobody can tell you

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.306
what's actually happening inside. A

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.360
chatbot predicts the next word.

00:02:31.306 --> 00:02:34.080
>> [music]

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.920
>> An agent decides the next action. Here's

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.760
how a chatbot actually works. It's a

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.720
large language model. When you type a

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.920
question, it's going to break that

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.200
question into small units of words

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.560
called tokens and it converts them into

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:50.200
numbers. And then it just finishes the

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:53.576
sentence. So, if you said, "Jack fell

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:56.240
down and broke his crown." Now, you know

00:02:53.576 --> 00:02:58.920
[music] that, but LLM does not know that

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:00.840
rhyme. It will know words like bones and

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:03.519
heart and crown. [music]

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:06.000
And all of those words could make sense,

00:03:03.519 --> 00:03:08.680
but based on its training, it predicts

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.680
that the most likely next word, given

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:13.760
that line, is crown. It's based on

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:17.160
probabilities. The agent has the same

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:19.160
language model in the center, but now

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:23.320
there are four workers around [music]

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:25.017
it. Analyst, planner, operator, auditor.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:25.080
One finds the pattern,

00:03:25.017 --> 00:03:28.200
>> [music]

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:30.400
>> one decides the plan, one does the work,

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:32.600
one checks the results. Let's make this

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:35.160
real. You can give an agent some

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:37.920
instruction like, "Every Monday at 7:00

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:40.920
a.m., review the past week's customer

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:44.040
support tickets, sales notes, and

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:46.440
product feedback. Identify three biggest

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:50.120
recurring issues, summarize what

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:52.080
changed, and email my leadership team a

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:54.600
one-page weekly brief." That's [music]

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:57.400
it. I mean, those are lots of steps, but

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:59.760
the agent will read the tickets, notes,

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.520
and feedback, and find the pattern

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.720
analyst. [music] It will then decide

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.560
what matters most and what belongs in

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:09.360
the brief planner. It will write and

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:12.040
send the update operator. And then it'll

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.640
check for weak logic, missing context,

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:17.280
or sloppy conclusions, and it'll refine

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:20.120
it auditor. Now, by Monday morning, the

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:21.720
brief is in your team's inbox. You did

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:24.440
not write the report, you did not

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:28.200
analyze it, you just assigned the job of

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:31.520
four people to one agent. So, here's

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:34.448
your move. Tonight, open ChatGPT agent

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.480
mode and give it one recurring task,

00:04:34.448 --> 00:04:36.880
>> [music]

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:39.720
>> but then watch what it does. You'll see

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:43.320
all four workers show up in real [music]

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:46.240
time. That's the anatomy of an AI agent.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:48.720
Now that you understand the parts, let's

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:51.600
look at the entire loop. The best thing

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:54.160
about agents is that they can adapt when

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.440
things go wrong. This is what makes

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:58.680
agents genuinely different from

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:01.800
everything that came before it. In the

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:04.200
1970s, there was an Air Force Colonel

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:06.840
John Boyd, and he studied a very

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:09.560
intriguing puzzle from the Korean War.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:12.880
American pilots in their F-86 kept

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:15.480
beating technically superior Soviet MiG.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:18.680
Now, the MiG was faster and it could

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:21.160
climb higher. It should have won, but it

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:23.000
didn't. And Boyd eventually found the

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:25.160
difference. The American pilots could

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:28.200
see more from their cockpits, and they

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:30.760
could adapt faster. So, they got inside

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:33.400
the enemy's decision cycle before the

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:37.520
enemy could respond. He called that loop

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:39.640
the OODA loop: observe, orient, decide,

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:41.280
act. [music] And in the world of agents,

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:43.640
it's the same thing. That is the real

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:46.280
test of an agent. When the obvious path

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:48.680
fails, can it choose a better one? Can

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:50.680
it go through its own UDA loop? So, let

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:53.080
me give you a concrete example. You can

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:54.880
build an automated workflow. Every

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:58.000
Friday, check this [music] week's

00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:00.080
grocery prices, build my shopping list,

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.240
and place the order. It [music] works

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:04.840
every Friday. Until one week, your usual

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.640
item is out of stock, [music]

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:09.880
and you have six friends coming for

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:12.160
dinner on Saturday. So, that automated

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:14.600
workflow is going to break. Not because

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:17.240
it's dumb, but because it's designed to

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:19.800
be obedient. It's designed to not think

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:21.800
on its own. An agent, on the other hand,

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:23.800
does something very unique. It sees the

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:26.480
usual list. It sees that it's not

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:28.919
working. It finds substitutes. It

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:32.000
adjusts quantities for six people. It

00:06:28.919 --> 00:06:34.200
checks your calendar, sees the dinner,

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.120
and rebuilds the entire order around it.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:39.480
A workflow can follow the process. An

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:41.480
agent can reroute it completely. [music]

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:43.400
That's the difference. So, when someone

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:46.280
says they built an agent, ask one

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:48.040
question. When the first path breaks,

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:50.000
does the agent keep following the

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:52.960
script, or can it find [music] a better

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:56.240
path? Can it find another way? That's

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:58.880
the agent adapting in real time. So,

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.800
this begs the question, right? If agents

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:02.479
have autonomy, why do they still fail so

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:02.600
often

00:07:02.479 --> 00:07:04.120
>> [music]

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:06.360
>> in real life?

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:08.480
That's next. The most dangerous thing

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:10.480
about AI agents is that they will do

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:13.160
wrong things faster and with more

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:15.960
confidence than you ever could. An agent

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:18.400
is not magic. It's a multiplier. I was

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:20.800
working with the aboard and leadership

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:22.600
team of large consumer company. Yeah,

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:25.919
I'm still working with them. And they're

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:28.040
profitable, well-run, great CEO. And

00:07:25.919 --> 00:07:30.840
when I asked what was stopping them from

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:35.000
using AI to drive customer acquisition,

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:37.480
for example, the CMO responded, "We have

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:41.080
all the data, but we'll still need to

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:43.320
build a clean process, so we can turn

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:46.040
that into something useful, something

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:48.480
insightful. And I asked where the real

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:50.960
challenge was and she said, "You know,

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:53.160
we need the right people in the seats

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:56.320
first." That's the story everywhere.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:59.160
Most AI problems are human problems in

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:01.480
disguise. An agent is just a mirror. It

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:03.960
reflects the quality of your thinking

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:07.560
back at you. It just amplifies it. Give

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:10.120
an agent vague goals, sloppy directions,

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:12.280
and no way to get feedback and it will

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:14.919
drive the car straight into the tree

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:17.120
faster and with more confidence than you

00:08:14.919 --> 00:08:20.160
ever could. Here's the dangerous part.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:22.760
An agent doesn't fix bad thinking, it

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:25.480
formalizes it. Usually the agent fails

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:27.120
because the human was vague, not because

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:29.240
the underlying model was bad or

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:33.039
anything. So, before you automate

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:36.080
anything, run a GPS check. Goal, proof,

00:08:33.039 --> 00:08:38.120
steps. Goal, can I define the goal in

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:41.360
one sentence very clearly? [music]

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:44.200
Proof, can I tell what good looks like

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:47.160
and how will I know if the agent got it

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:49.360
right? And steps, can I describe each

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:51.320
and every step very clearly without a

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:53.880
lot of hand-waving? Unless you can do

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:55.480
those three things very well, your agent

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:57.080
is not going to make any difference.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:59.040
I'll give [music] you an example. Here

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:01.360
are two instructions for your agent.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:02.880
First one, "Summarize my emails every

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:05.280
morning." It's good. And here's the

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:07.720
second one, "Every morning at 7:00 a.m.,

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:10.640
read my unread emails, categorize them

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:13.640
by urgency, draft replies to routine

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:15.440
messages, and flag anything from my top

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:16.680
five customers." So you see there is a

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:19.200
difference between those two

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:21.480
instructions and that [music] gap is

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:23.800
exactly where the mess lives. The

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:25.160
winners who can wield the power of AI

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:27.400
agents aren't just [music] going to be

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:30.400
engineers. They'll be the people who

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:31.839
understand their work deeply enough to

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:34.640
define it precisely. [music]

00:09:31.839 --> 00:09:37.000
Most companies want AI everywhere. The

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:39.440
ones actually winning are obsessively

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:41.560
narrow. If clarity is a bottleneck, then

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:44.560
the opportunity is not broad

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:47.680
intelligence, it's narrow ownership. I

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:49.200
was visiting the customer conference of

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:51.360
construction software company that I

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:54.091
work with, and the product lead was on

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:54.440
stage showing a demo of a single agent

00:09:54.091 --> 00:09:56.200
>> [music]

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:59.240
>> that was focused on a very specific

00:09:56.200 --> 00:10:02.160
problem, collecting field data for a

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:04.160
specific type of customers in a specific

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:07.240
type of situation. And it was a beta

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:09.720
launch. The demo worked mostly with a

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:12.480
few minor glitches here and there, but

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:14.560
when he showed the QR code at the end,

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:16.960
every hand in the conference room went

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:17.760
up with their phones. Everyone took a

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:20.960
picture [music]

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:23.800
because it solved a very specific, but

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:25.760
very real pain they all had been living

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:28.760
with for decades. [music] That is where

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:30.840
the real opportunity is, in your career

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:34.360
or in your company. Narrow [music]

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:36.160
focus. Here's the test. Find a highly

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:38.440
specific task [music]

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:40.520
people hate doing, but they have to do

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:42.920
it repeatedly. That's where the money

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:45.640
is. You know, we're entering an age

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:48.080
where we'll have more agents than human

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:50.680
beings on this planet. On the business

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:53.280
side, for every software company that

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:55.320
exists today, there will be an agent

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:58.480
company trying to dethrone it. The

00:10:55.320 --> 00:11:00.720
winners won't build the broadest agents

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:03.200
first. They'll build the one that

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:06.280
understands one workflow, [music]

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:08.920
one market, and one kind of user pain

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:10.680
better than everyone else. By the way,

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:13.400
if you want to keep this conversation

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:16.600
going, I write a short newsletter once a

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:18.880
week, just useful ideas, tools, honest

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:22.200
reflections. You can subscribe below.

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:24.680
It's free. AI will reshape almost every

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:27.720
role, but it won't replace what makes

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:30.960
you irreplaceable. You know, AI is a

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:33.920
giant decoupling machine. For most of

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:35.560
our modern history, your income was tied

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:37.720
to your hours. [music]

00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:40.840
Even at the top, you're always trading

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:43.120
time for decisions. Agents are breaking

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:45.920
that link for the first time. Now they

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:48.480
do the work and you scale your judgment

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:51.320
in areas where it matters most. And that

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:54.480
changes what's valuable. We're entering

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:57.800
an era of infinite output, content,

00:11:54.480 --> 00:12:00.400
code, and analysis all becoming super

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:02.800
cheap. When intelligence becomes that

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:05.280
cheap, judgment becomes even more

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:07.520
expensive. When output becomes infinite,

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:10.160
taste becomes scarce. Every time you

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:12.080
define a task clearly enough for an

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:15.280
agent to run it, you're not just

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:18.640
training the system, you're clarifying

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:21.680
your own standards. You're learning what

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:24.560
good actually looks like. Sure, some

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:27.480
roles will be reshaped. The paralegal,

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:29.800
the junior analyst, the coordinator. But

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:31.800
every disruption has always created new

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:34.000
roles that never existed before. [music]

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:36.920
Before the internet, nobody imagined the

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:39.280
role of an online community manager. The

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:42.760
question is not whether the shift

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:45.440
happens. It is whether you shape it or

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:48.323
get shaped by it. The most valuable

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:50.720
person is no longer the one who can

00:12:48.323 --> 00:12:54.920
[music] think the fastest. It's the one

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:58.080
who can define good work, spot bad work,

00:12:54.920 --> 00:13:00.600
and know when to trust an agent and when

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:04.040
to trust a human. That is where your

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:07.120
value is going to move. Ironically, AI

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:08.320
will make human life less robotic.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:11.000
Thank you.

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:11.000
And I love you.

